      "textEn": "282\ngoes. Ashwatthama was the legitimate son of Dronacharya and Kripi, but he had made his life fallen.\nTherefore, it was appropriate to treat him not like a Brahmin, but like an animal. Shibiray ninishantam rajva bandhvam ripun balat. Praharjunan Prakrutito Bhagavanambujekshanah. 24). Meaning of the word:\nShibiraya- at the camp of the army; Ninishantam- bringing; Rjva- with ropes; Badadhwa- bound; Ripum- to the enemy;\nBalat- by force; Praha- said; Arjunam- to Arjun; Prakupitah- angered; Bhagavan- God; Ambuj- with lotus-like eyes;\nAfter binding Ashwatthama, Arjun wanted to take him towards the camp of the army\nThat while looking with his lotus-like eyes, Lord Sri Krishna said to the angry Arjun in this way.\nMeaning: Here, Arjuna and Lord Krishna are both shown as angry, but Arjuna's eyes were like red copper-hot balls, while Lord Kau's eyes were lotus-like.\nIts meaning is that Arjuna and Lord's angry forms are not the same. Lord is divine and is complete in any\nstate. Their anger is not like the anger of bound beings subdued by the qualities of material nature.\nIs not. Because they are complete, therefore their anger and their happiness are the same. Their\nanger does not manifest in the three qualities of nature. This is merely a symbol of the inclination of their minds for the benefit of their devotees, because that is their divine nature. Therefore, even when they are angry,\nthe one who is worthy of anger becomes blessed. They remain unchanged in all conditions.\nI have come to protect this Brahmajanma, to kill the enemy.\nHe who sleeps, who sleeps, who sleeps, the children. 35). Meaning of the word:\nMa enam-- Never to him; Partha-- Arjuna; Arhasi-- You want; Tratum-- To free; Brahma-bandham-- Brahmin;\nRelating to; immu-- This; Jahi- to kill; Yah-- He who; Asau-- He who is; Anagasah-- without fault; Suptan-- sleeping;\nAvadhit-- of killing; Nishi-- at night; Balakan-- of children."
