      "textEn": "283\n\nLord Shri Krishna said: Hey Arjun, do not show mercy except for this Brahma-Bandhu.\nShow mercy, because he has killed sleeping innocent children. Note: The term Brahma-Bandhu is important. The person who is born in a Brahmin family, but\ndoes not have the ability to be called a Brahmin, is addressed as a Brahmin-kin, not\nthat a Brahmin. The son of a High Court judge does not automatically become a judge, but\nthere is no harm in calling the son of a judge a relative of the judge. Therefore, just as someone\ndoes not become a judge of the court by birth, similarly, someone is not a Brahmin by birth alone, but he has to acquire the necessary qualifications of a Brahmin. Just as\nthe position of a judge is for a person worthy of it, similarly, the position of a Brahmin is also obtained only through merit.\nIt is the order of the scriptures that if a man born in a family other than that of a Brahmin\nhas the virtues of a Brahmin, then he has to be accepted as a Brahmin. Similarly, if\na person born in a Brahmin family is devoid of the qualities of a Brahmin, then he should be called an Abrahman or, to put it correctly, a Brahmin-Bandhu. The supreme proof of all the Vedas, Lord Sri Krishna has said this.\nHe himself has told the difference, and he is going to tell the reason for this in the next verse.\nMattam pramattam unmattam suptam balam khiyam jadam. The one who is devoted, fearless, and not afraid of enemies, is a knower of dharma. 36). Meaning of the word:\nMattam- careless; Pramatamu- one who is intoxicated; Unmattam- mad; Suptam- one who is sleeping; Balam- a child;\nStriyam- a woman; Jadam- a fool; Prapannam- one who surrenders; Viratham- one without a chariot; Bhitam- one who is afraid;\nNa- no; Ripum- to the enemy; Hanti- they kill; Dharmavit- one who knows the rules of dharma.\n.\n\nThe man who knows the principles of religion does not kill such an enemy who\nis careless, intoxicated, mad, asleep, frightened, or without a chariot. N