      "textEn": "285\nIt is not the food of man. The main duty of a civilized man is to return to the abode of God,\nto make himself worthy. He says that behind the slaughter of animals, there is a systematic conspiracy of sinful gangs.\nTherefore, they are punishable in the same way as a conspiratorial gang\nwho collectively murder a human being. He who commands the slaughter of animals, who TY\ncommits slaughter; who sells the slaughtered animals, who rots the animals; who distributes such edible\nthings, and that too; who eats such edible things; all of them are murderers, and all of them\nare subject to punishment by the laws of nature / no matter how much scientific progress one makes,\nhe cannot create life; therefore, no one has the right to arbitrarily kill any living being. For carnivores, only limited animal sacrifices are permitted in the scriptures, and\nsuch permission is also to restrict the opening of slaughterhouses, not to\nencourage animal slaughter.\nThe method under which animal sacrifice is permitted in the scriptures is\nin the interest of both the animals to be sacrificed and the animal-eaters. This is for the benefit of the animal,\nbecause the animal that is to be sacrificed, as soon as it is sacrificed on the altar, attains a human form, and the animal-eater\nis also saved from gross sins.\nThe slaughterhouses, which are such disgusting places\nthat are being run in a well-planned manner, are places for all kinds of material evils to flourish for society, the nation, and the general public. Consuming the meat given by them is the same gross sin. This material\nworld itself is a place filled with worries, and by encouraging the slaughter of animals in it,\nthe whole environment is filled with war, epidemics, It becomes increasingly polluted by famine and many other unwanted calamities. Having heard from you, I have heard from Panchalya.\nI will offer my head to him, O king, who is the son of Mani. 38>."
