      "textEn": "287\nHe who commits abduction is liable to death. Krishna reminded Arjun of this fact, so that he would pay attention to those\nand act accordingly. Suta said:\nThus, tested, Dharma, O Partha, was spoken by Krishna. To destroy the bad, the son of the Guru, even if it is self-harm, is great. 40>. Meaning of the word:\nSuta-- Suta Goswami said; Uvacha-- said; Evam-- this; Parikshita-- while examining; Dharamam-- in matters of duty; Parthah--\nShri Arjun; Krishnena-- by Lord Krishna; Choditah-- being encouraged; Na Echhat-- not wanted; Hantum-- to kill;\nGuru-Sutam-- to the son of one's guru; Yadyapi-- although; Atma-Hanam-- the slayer of sons; Mahan-- very great;\n\nSuta Goswami said: Although, while testing Arjuna in religion, Krishna encouraged him to\nkill the son of Dronacharya, but Mahatma Arjuna did not like this\nthought, although Ashwatthama was a brutal murderer of Arjuna's family members. Meaning: Arjun was undoubtedly a great man, as is proven here.\nHere, God\nKrishna himself encourages him to kill Drona's son, but Arjun thinks that\nhe should give his life to the son of his great guru, because even though he is the unworthy son of his guru, he has committed such heinous acts out of his whim that were not in anyone's best interest. But\nafter all, he is the son of Dronacharya. Lord Krishna encouraged Arjuna from outside, because he wanted to test Arjuna's\nduty-fulness. It was not that Arjuna did not have a complete understanding of his duty,\nnor was Lord Krishna unaware of Arjuna's sense of duty. But Shri Krishna\ntook the test of his many pure devotees to enhance their sense of duty. The Gopis\nwere also subjected to similar tests. The test of the Maharaja was also taken. But all pure devotees\nFulfill their respective tests taken by God."
