      "textEn": "295\n\nNakula, Sahadeva, Yuyudhana, Dhananjaya.\nLord, the son of Devaki, and others, and all the women. 50>. Meaning of the word:\nNakula - Nakula; Sahadeva - Sahadeva; Cha - and; Yuyudhana - Satyaki; Dhananjaya - Arjuna; Bhagavan - Bhagavan; Devako -\nSon - Devaki's son, Lord Sri Krishna; Ye - who; Cha - and; Anye - others; Yah - they; Cha - and; Yoshitah - women.\n\nNakula and Sahadeva (the king's younger brothers) and Satyaki, Arjuna, Devaki's son, Lord\nSri Krishna and women and others unanimously expressed their agreement with the king.\nTatrahaamarshito bhimastasya shreyaan vadhaah smrutah. He who, with his own self, speaks of another's self, is sleeping and waking, vain. 51). Meaning of the word:\nThere- after that; Aha- said; Amarshitah- in an angry tone; Bhimah- Bhima; Tasya- his; Shreyan- supreme welfare;\nVadha- slaughter; Smrutah- remembered; Na- not; Bhartuh- of the husband; Na- not; Atmanah- of oneself; Cha- and; Arthae- for; Yah- who; Ahn- struck; Suptaan- sleeping; Shishun- to the children; Vritha- in vain.\nBut Bhima, who was in a furious state of mind, did not agree with them, and he recommended the death of that guilty one,\nwho had killed the sleeping children in vain.\nIn which neither his own nor his master's well-being, om)\nNishamya Bhimagaditam Draupadiyascha Chaturbhujah.\nSeeing this, he spoke to his friend with a smile. 52). Shabdartham\nNishamya-- Hearing; Bhima-- by Bhima; Gaditamu-- said; Draupadiyaah-- of Draupadi; Cha- tatha; Chatuh-bhujah-- four; Bhujah-- having four arms; Alokya- seeing; Vadanam- face; Sakhyuh- of one's friend; Idam- this; Aah- said;\nHasan- while laughing; Iva- as if;\nBhagavan- the Lord;\nNishamya- having heard;\nBhima- by Bhima;\nGaditamu- having said;\nDraupadiyaah- of Draupadi;\nCha- tatha; Chatuh-bhujah- four; Bhujah- having four arms;\nAlokya- having seen; Vadanam- face; Sakhyuh- of one's friend; Idam- this; Aah- said;\nBhagavan- the Lord;\nNishamya- having heard.\n\nHearing the words of Bh