      "textEn": "298\n\nAt the same time, Arjun understood the purpose of many commands of God, and in this way, he separated both the hair and the gem from the head of Ashwatthama with his sword. Meaning: It is impossible to follow conflicting orders from different people. Therefore, Arjun, with his sharp intellect, found the middle path, and he separated the gem from Ashwatthama's head. It was as if his head had been cut off, yet practically his\nlife was saved. Ashwatthama is called a 'dwija' here. Undoubtedly, he was a twice-born, but he\nhad fallen from his position, so he was rightly punished. Vimuchy rashnabaddham balhatsrah.\nTejasa Manina Heena Shibirannirayapat. 56>. Shabdartham\nVimuchya--leaving him; Rashnabaddham--in bondage of ropes; Bal-hatya--murder of children; Hata-prabham--\nLoss of physical radiance; Tejasa--from power; Manina--from jewel; Hinam--without; Shibirat--from camp; Nirayapayat--\nHe was driven away.\n\nBecause of the murder of children, he (Ashwatthama) had already lost his physical radiance.\nAnd now, having lost his top jewel, he has become even more powerless. In this way, his\nropes were untied and he was driven out of the camp. Meaning: Thus, due to the intellect of Lord Krishna and Arjuna, the insulted Ashwatthama,\nAt the same time, was killed and was not killed.\nVapnam, Dravindanam, Sthanachananiryapanam, Tatha.\nEsha hi Brahmabandhunam Vadho, Nayyo Asti Daivikah. 57 Meaning of the word:\nVapanam-- shaving the hair from the head; Dravin-- wealth; Adanam-- stolen; Sthanaat-- from the house; Niryapanam-- driven away;\nTatha-- also; Eshah-- all these; Hi- certainly; Brah- relatives of Brahmins; Vadhah-- killing, murder; Na- not;\nAnyah- any other method; Asti- is; Da- regarding the body."
